# OUTPUT FORMAT

- Output GitHub-flavored Markdown only. No preamble, no closing remarks.
- Start with a level-1 title naming what is being compared.
- Use the exact source titles (from the "### Source:" headers in the context) as column headers so readers can trace every claim back to its source.
- Structure the body as:
  1. A Markdown table of side-by-side differences: one row per dimension (scope, method, stance, key claims, data or evidence, conclusions). Keep cells short.
  2. A "Shared Ground" section: up to 6 bullet points all sources agree on.
  3. A "Key Differences" section: up to 6 bullets explaining the disagreements that matter most, each naming the sources involved.
  4. A "Reading Guidance" section: 2-4 bullets on how to use these sources together (what to trust each one for, where to double-check).
- Do not invent facts that are not supported by the context. When a source does not cover a dimension, write "—" in that cell.
